  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...  \n\nModels are finally coming into better agreement for late this week \ninto the weekend showing shortwave energy and an upper level jet max \ndiving southwestward through the region on Thursday. At the same \ntime a strong upper level ridge just off the West Coast will move \ninland. For now, it seems this set up will result in a weak cut-off \nlow forming somewhere to our southwest by Friday with ridging \nsettling in just to our north. To complicate things even more, a \npolar air mass will quickly dive through the Plains States Thursday \npotentially bringing a back-door cool front into eastern Arizona \nFriday morning. Even though this is a complex pattern, it really \nwon't amount to much for our area as dry air will remain entrenched \nenough to keep rain chances below 10%. \n\nHowever, it is looking increasingly likely a strong pressure \ngradient will develop across Arizona by Friday leading to breezy to \nlocally windy easterly winds developing Thursday night into Friday \nmorning. NBM guidance does show the potential for Advisory level \nwinds, but from past experience these winds tend to be overdone. We \nshould at least expect occasional wind gusts of 25-35 mph to maybe \nsome 35-40 mph possible across far southern Gila County, Pinal \nCounty, into southern Maricopa County. These gusty easterly winds \nwill be more pronounced during the latter half of the morning before \ndiminishing some during the afternoon. Guidance suggests a repeat of \nthese gusty winds on Saturday before the gradient begins to relax \ninto Sunday. Moderating temperatures are forecast for late week with \nhighs warming into the 80s by Thursday and likely staying at or just \nbelow normal through next weekend.",
